---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const chartCode = `const bars = chart({
  mark: "bar",
  data,
  encoding: { x: "category", y: "value" },
});`;

const stackCode = `const row = stackX([leftBars, matrix, details]);
const view = stackY([topBars, row]);

// Optional refinements for a specific design:
const alignedView = stackY([topBars, row], {
  align: [matrix, matrix],
});

const linkedRow = stackX([horizontalBars, pac], {
  link: true,
});`;

const repeatCode = `const pies = repeatX(ids, (id) =>
  chart({
    mark: "pie",
    data: detailsById[id],
    encoding: { x: "category", y: "value" },
  }),
);`;

const embedCode = `const repeated = repeat(events, (event) =>
  chart({
    mark: "bubble",
    data: event.points,
    encoding: { x: "x", y: "y", size: "size" },
  }),
);

const view = embed(
  sequenceContainer({ xDomain: weeks, yDomain: learners }),
  repeated,
  { x: "week", y: "learner", key: "id", width: 48, height: 48 },
);`;

const containerCode = `const grid = gridContainer({
  rowDomain: cohorts,
  columnDomain: metrics,
  row: "cohort",
  column: "metric",
});

const customGrid = customContainer({
  width: 480,
  height: 240,
  slots(data) {
    return data.map((datum, index) => ({
      datum,
      key: datum.id,
      x: 40 + index * 64,
      y: 120,
    }));
  },
});`;

const annotationCode = `const label = text({
  text: "Intersections",
  width: 120,
  height: 24,
  fontWeight: 700,
});

const flag = image({
  url: "https://kapowaz.github.io/circle-flags/flags/us.svg",
  width: 28,
  height: 28,
});

const boxed = frame(stackY([label, matrix]), {
  padding: 8,
  stroke: "#94a3b8",
});`;

const customCode = `const timeline = custom(
  {
    width: 320,
    height: 80,
    options: { width: 320, height: 80 },
    render(svg, { width }) {
      d3.select(svg)
        .append("line")
        .attr("x1", 0)
        .attr("x2", width)
        .attr("y1", 40)
        .attr("y2", 40)
        .attr("stroke", "#172033");
    },
  },
  { classTag: "timeline" },
);`;

const renderCode = `const root = document.querySelector("#app");
view.render(root);`;

const dataCode = `const csv = await loadCsvText(new URL("./data.csv", import.meta.url));
const rows = parseCsv(csv);
const columns = tableColumns(rows);
const measures = numericColumns(rows, { exclude: ["category"] });
const cells = crossJoin(rows, measures, (row, measure) => ({ row, measure }));`;

const validationCode = `validateChartConfig(config);

if (isLayoutNode(value)) {
  value.render(document.querySelector("#app"));
}

assertLayoutNode(value, "value");`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Composition API | composite.js">
  <div class="docs-content">
    <header class="docs-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">API</p>
      <h1>Factories for charts and their relationships</h1>
      <p>
        The primary API defines charts and abstract composition relationships.
        The resulting nodes are measured, positioned, and rendered by the
        layout system.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Default-first composition</h2>
      <p>
        Begin with the required chart data, encodings, and composition
        operators. Sizes, margins, repeat padding, and alignment all have
        default behavior. Their options are available for deliberate overrides,
        but they are not required to produce a complete layout.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2><code>chart(config)</code></h2>
      <p>
        Creates a chart leaf node. The config includes <code>mark</code>,
        <code>data</code>, and <code>encoding</code>. Sizing, color, padding,
        label, and axis options supported by the selected mark are optional.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{chartCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2><code>stackX(nodes, options)</code> and <code>stackY(nodes, options)</code></h2>
      <p>
        Express a left-to-right or top-to-bottom relationship between layout
        nodes. Pass only layout nodes, not raw chart instances. Concrete
        positions and dimensions are computed automatically. To override the
        default gap, pass <code>margin</code> as one number or a list with
        <code>nodes.length - 1</code> values. Use
        <code>align</code> when a stack child should align to a nested node.
        Use <code>link: true</code> on a two-chart stack to draw connector
        lines between matching values: <code>stackX</code> matches
        <code>encoding.y</code>, and <code>stackY</code> matches
        <code>encoding.x</code>. Supported link marks are <code>bar</code>,
        <code>pac</code>, and <code>scatter</code>.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{stackCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2><code>repeatX(domain, fn, options)</code> and <code>repeatY(domain, fn, options)</code></h2>
      <p>
        Express that one child view repeats for every domain value along an
        axis. The factory callback receives each value and returns a layout
        node. Defaults provide repeat sizing and padding; use
        <code>paddingInner</code>, <code>paddingOuter</code>, <code>width</code>,
        and <code>height</code> to tune repeat sizing.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{repeatCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>
        <code>repeat(domain, fn, options)</code>, <code>embed(container, repeated, mapping)</code>,
        and <code>sequenceContainer(options)</code>
      </h2>
      <p>
        Use directionless repeats when children should be placed into container
        slots. <code>sequenceContainer()</code> creates slots from
        <code>xDomain</code> and <code>yDomain</code>; <code>embed()</code>
        maps repeated data fields onto those slots.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{embedCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>
        <code>gridContainer(options)</code> and <code>customContainer(options)</code>
      </h2>
      <p>
        <code>gridContainer()</code> creates row-and-column slots for embedded
        children. <code>customContainer()</code> is the extension point for
        user-defined slot placement and optional structural rendering. The
        exported <code>GridContainer</code> and <code>SequenceContainer</code>
        constructors provide class-based equivalents to their factory helpers.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{containerCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>
        <code>text(config)</code>, <code>image(config)</code>,
        <code>custom(renderable, options)</code>, and <code>frame(node, options)</code>
      </h2>
      <p>
        <code>text()</code> creates SVG text that participates in layout.
        <code>image()</code> creates an SVG image leaf from a URL, with
        sizing, fitting, clipping, opacity, and transform options.
        <code>custom()</code> wraps your own renderable object when an example
        needs a specialized D3 or SVG drawing while still participating in
        composition.
        <code>frame()</code> wraps an existing layout node with padding and a
        border, which is useful for grouping or highlighting a composed region.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{annotationCode}</code></pre>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{customCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2><code>.render(container, options)</code></h2>
      <p>
        Render any layout node into a browser DOM element with
        <code>.render()</code>. The library creates an SVG subtree inside the
        container and computes layout from the composed node tree. Optional
        render dimensions can override the computed size when necessary.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{renderCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Data helpers</h2>
      <p>
        <code>loadCsvText()</code> loads CSV text, <code>parseCsv()</code>
        parses it into rows, and <code>tableColumns()</code> and
        <code>numericColumns()</code> inspect the resulting table.
        <code>crossJoin()</code> builds a Cartesian product, optionally mapping
        each pair into a new value.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{dataCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Validation and node guards</h2>
      <p>
        <code>validateChartConfig()</code> checks a chart configuration without
        rendering it. <code>isLayoutNode()</code> tests unknown values, while
        <code>assertLayoutNode()</code> throws a descriptive error for invalid
        composition inputs.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{validationCode}</code></pre>
    </section>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
